{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cfw8WErPqsEJAUcNKEuCL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Battery bank\n\nLiFePO4 tolerates deep, regular discharge (unlike lead-acid, which needs ~50% DoD for longevity) — 80% DoD is a safe design point that still gives long cycle life (thousands of cycles).\n\n- Energy needed for 3 days of autonomy: 1.5 kWh/day × 3 = 4.5 kWh usable\n- Nominal capacity = 4.5 kWh ÷ 0.80 DoD = **5.6 kWh**, round to **~6 kWh nominal** (e.g., a 48V 100Ah pack, or two 51.2V ~57Ah units)\n\nThis leaves margin so you're rarely pushing past 80% DoD even on a run of bad weather.\n\n## Solar array\n\nThe governing constraint for a year-round cabin isn't the annual average sun — it's the **worst month**, because that's when you must still fully recharge the battery and carry the load.\n\n**Step 1 — worst-month sun resource.** At 35°N in New Mexico high desert (e.g., Albuquerque/Santa Fe area), this is an excellent solar resource — but even there, December is the low point. With a winter-biased tilt (latitude + ~15°, i.e. ~50°) rather than a flat/latitude tilt, worst-month peak sun hours run roughly **4.5–5 h/day equivalent** (NREL PVWatts-type data for this region). I'd design to the conservative end: **4.5 h/day**.\n\n**Step 2 — system losses** (array nameplate → usable battery energy):\n- MPPT charge controller: ~97%\n- Inverter (if any AC loads): ~94%\n- Wiring/connections: ~98%\n- Desert dust/soiling: ~96% (worth being a bit pessimistic here — dust accumulation between rains is a real factor)\n- Battery charge efficiency, LiFePO4: ~97%\n\nCombined: 0.97 × 0.94 × 0.98 × 0.96 × 0.97 ≈ **0.83**. I'd round down to **0.75** to also absorb aging (LiFePO4 panels/electronics degrade slowly, but 10 years of 0.5%/yr panel degradation plus real-world non-ideal tilt/orientation days adds up).\n\n**Step 3 — required array size:**\n\nArray (W) = Daily load (Wh) ÷ (worst-month PSH × system efficiency)\n= 1500 Wh ÷ (4.5 h × 0.75)\n= 1500 ÷ 3.375\n≈ **444 W**\n\n**Step 4 — margin.** Add ~25–30% headroom for: cloudy-day strings before full recovery, load creep (you will add devices), and the fact that worst-month estimates are themselves uncertain without a site-specific PVWatts run.\n\n444 W × 1.3 ≈ **575 W → round to a practical ~600 W array** (e.g., two 300 W panels or three 200 W panels).\n\n## Bottom line\n\n| Component | Size |\n|---|---|\n| Solar array | **~600 W** (STC nameplate), tilted steeply (~50°) for winter bias |\n| LiFePO4 battery bank | **~6 kWh nominal** (≈4.5 kWh usable at 80% DoD) |\n\n**Caveats worth flagging:**\n- The worst-month PSH figure (4.5 h) is a reasoned estimate for this latitude/climate, not a pulled site-specific number — before buying hardware, run your actual coordinates through NREL PVWatts with your intended tilt/azimuth to replace that assumption.\n- This sizes for *recovery each clear day*, not for multiple consecutive overcast days in a row — true multi-day storms are rare but not impossible in NM winter; if you want resilience against that, the battery (not the array) is the cheap lever to oversize further.\n- 1.5 kWh/day is a light load (lighting + electronics, no fridge/well pump implied) — if those get added later, the array is the harder thing to resize, so there's an argument for buying one extra panel (~300 W) now rather than later."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21838,"cache_read_input_tokens":19009,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21838},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b5bbf2e0-34ca-40d5-87ae-b45f334c72d2","uuid":"8e948ac2-681a-4608-8024-0c11913edebf","timestamp":"2026-10-11T21:06:47.953Z","request_id":"req_011Cfw8WEbFx